// The boss journal: the Hydra of the Drowned Temple, Mother of Nine Heads, in each hero's own words (docs/plans/journal-voices.md)
(window.JOURNAL_DATA = window.JOURNAL_DATA || {}).hydra = {
  // the first time this hero meets her (after the first pull)
  meet: {
    bastion: "The Hydra never moves. She sits in her pool and lets the heads do the fighting, each from its own column. I held her body and thought that was enough. Every head is its own line to watch. Gave ground.",
    gloomsworn: "The Mother keeps her watch from the pool, and her heads keep theirs from the columns, each awake when the others sleep. I cut the first one that bit me, and it was the cruellest. The gentle one goes first. The dark remembers.",
    thresher: "The Hydra sits in her pool like a flooded field, heads coming up round her like weeds after rain. I cut the rankest first and it came back bigger at the end. Pull the mildest first, and burn every stump. Not yet ripe.",
    bloodreaver: "The Mother of Nine Heads, and every one of them bites! I cut the meanest neck first because it had hurt me best, and it came back bigger at the end. Lesson: the gentlest dies first. The old girl and I are not finished.",
    runeguard: "They call her the Mother of Nine Heads, and the Hydra keeps strict order: the heads speak in turn, sunwise from the north. I chose by anger, not reason; the first cut returns at the end, larger. Not foreseen; now it is.",
    gorgemaw: "My hostess serves a whole table of heads, each with its own seasoning: acid, fire, ice, storm. I carved the spiciest first, greedy as ever, and it came back as a larger portion at the end. Start with the mildest. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "The Matriarch holds the drowned temple by right of birth, and each of her heads is a separate party to the claim. I struck the hardest first; the fine print says the first head cut is reissued at the close, enlarged. Breach noted.",
    ironpalm: "She sat in the water and did not move. Her heads spoke for her, one after another, round the ring. I answered the loudest first. It was the wrong one to answer. Breathe.",
    ranger: "Sits in a stagnant pool, never moves. Heads on long necks, each its own weather, each picks a target in turn round the ring of columns. Cut the hardest first. Wrong. Cut the softest. No line yet.",
    shadowblade: "The mark has more eyes than any mark deserves, all on necks, all looking somewhere different. I took the angriest head first, and it returned for an encore, larger. The gentle one goes first. Next time no eye will find me.",
    pyromancer: "The Mother of Nine Heads, in a puddle! I came to set a temple alight and found it flooded. I burned the Hydra's fiercest head first, which was wrong: the first one cut comes back bigger at the end. Gentlest first. Mostly unsinged, thoroughly damp.",
    thermist: "Cold water, stagnant, still. Then heads, so many, so hot-tempered, and I ran hot and we cut the angriest first. Wrong. The first one cut comes back bigger at the end. Gentlest first. Back to the middle.",
    driftblade: "The serpent sat in her pool and never once moved, which I do not understand, and I ran rings round her and cut the fiercest head first, which was wrong, the first one cut comes back bigger at the end, so gentlest first.",
    marionettist: "Our leading lady never leaves her pool, and keeps a whole chorus of heads to do her acting. I let the company cut the loudest head first; it returns, larger, for the finale. Cast off the gentlest first. (My Doll took notes.)",
    cleric: "The Mother of Nine Heads, sitting in her still pool like a widow at a wake. I mended after every bite instead of before, and ran dry. Then I learned that every stump must be seared. Dawn keep us.",
    grovekeeper: "The Mother of Nine Heads, rooted in her pond like an old willow. I chased every bite instead of planting ahead, and wilted. Then I saw it: a cut head comes back like bindweed unless the stump is seared.",
    chronomancer: "The Mother of Nine Heads bites in turn, round from the north, like a slow clock striking. I will have mended after every bite; I did, and ran dry. This time I learned that the order we cut them in comes back.",
    alembist: "Specimen: the Mother of Nine Heads, seated in a stagnant pool, never moves. Ailments: bites from every side, in turn. I brewed after each bite instead of before, and the shelves spoiled. Note: sear the stumps. A flask went off. Intended.",
    threadweaver: "The Tangle sits in her pool with her necks all knotted about her. My stitches cut the fiercest one first, and it came back bigger at the end. Unpick the gentlest first, next time. A sloppy seam, entirely ours.",
  },
  // the first time this hero beats her
  win: {
    bastion: "The Hydra is down. Every stump seared, every head answered in its order, and the line stood in the water the whole night through. The pool is still. Held.",
    gloomsworn: "The Mother has closed her eyes, every one of them. The pool lies black and still between the columns, and I sat the last of her watch for her. The watch is relieved.",
    thresher: "The Hydra has gone under her own water, and the temple can lie fallow. Every stump burned clean, like stubble after harvest, so nothing grows back. Something new in me, ripening.",
    bloodreaver: "The old girl sank and stayed sunk. Every head marked me, acid, fire, ice and all, and I wore each scar home. Finest collection I own. I will miss her.",
    runeguard: "The Hydra is judged. Every head spoke in its turn, and every stump answered in the order we wrote it; I kept that order in my head through the dark water. As foreseen, finally.",
    gorgemaw: "The banquet is over. My hostess went under her own soup with every head on the platter. Acid, fire, frost, storm: a tasting menu no cook will ever match. Magnificent. Still hungry.",
    oathbroker: "The Matriarch's claim on the temple is voided, every head discharged and every stump sealed. We kept the order of cutting on file, and the final instalment came due in her heart. Countersigned.",
    ironpalm: "Her heads spoke, one by one, and then all together. We answered each in turn. Then her heart spoke, and we answered last. The water is quiet. Breathe.",
    ranger: "The beast is down. Stumps all seared, pool gone still. Clean line, at last.",
    shadowblade: "The mark closed every one of its eyes at once, and the pool went quiet. All those heads, all that looking, and in the end none of them saw me. None of them ever did.",
    pyromancer: "The Hydra sank and stayed sunk, and every stump is seared shut! The temple steamed like a kettle and I glowed the whole time. My sleeves are soaked AND scorched, which I did not think possible.",
    thermist: "The Mother went under and did not rise. The pool went cold, properly cold, every stump seared. I ran hot and cold all evening and then, at last, neither. Back to the middle.",
    driftblade: "The serpent went under for the last time and the pool went flat and still, and I ran a last circle round every seared stump and through the columns and out into the night air, never landing, never once.",
    marionettist: "Final curtain for our leading lady. Every stump seared, every head dismissed, the company bowing on dry boards at last. (The Doll took the last bow, dripping. Standing ovation.)",
    cleric: "The Mother is still, and her pool with her. Every head that bit my flock is quiet now, and every friend walked out of that drowned temple. Thank the light for each of them.",
    grovekeeper: "The marsh-mother is down, and the still water will go to reeds in a season or so. My lot came through every head she grew. Plant first, and let the pond keep the rest.",
    chronomancer: "The Hydra fell, and every stump stayed quiet for once. I did not have to undo a single one of us. I will have been proud; I am now, and I expect I shall be again.",
    alembist: "Result: the specimen, still at last. Every patient alive, every stump seared, shelves bare. I took a vial of the pool water. Labelled: do not drink, do not heat, do not tell anyone.",
    threadweaver: "The Tangle is unpicked, every stump seared shut and every neck cut loose. The whole hem came home dry, more or less. Pulled it tight.",
  },
  moves: {
    "Mother's Cry": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She cried out and it reached every one of us, through the columns and the water alike. Nothing to block. The line came to it already mended, footing set, and took it standing. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Her cry finds every one of us, and no column hides us from it. It sounds again when she rises from the deep. I saw the watch whole before it came, and let it pass like the small hours.",
      thresher: "Her cry falls on the whole field at once, like hail with no hedge to hide behind. I made sure the mending was in before it came, not after. It comes again when she rises from under the water.",
      bloodreaver: "The old girl screamed and it went through every one of us, no cover anywhere. Barely a scratch for me, shameful, but my friends felt it, so we came to it mended, not after. She screams again when she rises from the dive.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the heads fall still and her throat swells. The answer: it reaches every corner and no ward turns it, so the mending goes on before the cry, not after; it sounds again as she surfaces. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess sends her cry to the whole table at once, and I cannot eat it for anyone. We came to it well fed rather than hungry afterwards. She serves it again when she comes up from below. Shrill.",
      oathbroker: "The Cry is a levy on all the undersigned at once, collected again when she surfaces from her dive. No clause excuses anyone; the fine print is to arrive already mended. Entered in the ledger.",
      ironpalm: "She spoke to all of us at once. There is no answering it. We came to it whole, and let it pass through us. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Cry. Reaches everyone, no cover. Mended before, not after. Comes again when she surfaces. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark screamed, and it found everyone, even me, which was rude. No shadow keeps it out, so I made sure I was whole before it sounded. It comes again when she rises from the water.",
      pyromancer: "The Hydra screamed through the whole temple and there was nowhere to hide from it, not even behind my own brilliance. The mending had to land before she opened her mouth, not after. Only my fringe curled.",
      thermist: "Her cry: everyone, no cover. Cold rule: be mended before it, not after. I was not, once, and ran scalding with fright. Steadied. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Her cry went through the whole temple and there was no running through it or round it, which offends me, so I came to it already mended and kept moving through the noise, never landing.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady's aria reaches every seat in the house, the wings included. No exits; the company came to it already mended and played through. (The Doll covered its ears.)",
      cleric: "The Mother cried out, and it reached every one of my flock with nowhere to hide. I laid the light on them all before it, not after. She cries again when she rises from the deep. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Her cry comes like a flood over a low field: no high ground, my lot all at once. Plant first. It comes again when she rises from the deep, so I keep seed back for that too.",
      chronomancer: "The cry reaches every one of us, and there is no hiding from it. I had everyone mended in the moment before; it sounds again on the beat she surfaces. Next time, which was last time, I'll be early.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the specimen's cry, felt by everyone, no hiding from it; again when she surfaces. Remedy: the big brew poured over the whole party just before, not after. Front shelf.",
      threadweaver: "Her cry ran along every thread at once and there was no tying it off. I had my stitches mended before she opened her mouth, not after. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Rending Maul': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "The great jaws came down on me, a wide sweep in front of her. I waited for the neck to draw back, shield up in the last instant, and the line stood clear of the sweep. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The maul is for whoever keeps the watch at her front, and that was me. I drew the dark about me just before the neck struck and took it whole. The others kept out of the sweep, where the night is kinder.",
      thresher: "Her maul comes down on our knight and sweeps wide in front of her, like a scythe on standing grain. I kept out of the swath, and he had his guard up before it landed.",
      bloodreaver: "The maul! A great sweep in front of her and the heaviest of it on me. A good one, deep. I had my guard ready, took it, then answered while it was fresh. The others kept out of the sweep.",
      runeguard: "The omen: the great neck coils back over the water. The answer: a ward laid a breath before it falls on me, and the others well outside the sweep before her. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "The maul is the main course, served to me alone with a wide sweep of gravy before her. I opened wide with my guard ready and kept my friends off my plate. Hearty.",
      oathbroker: "The Maul is payable by whoever holds her, and I accepted delivery behind every protection I had. The fine print is the width of the sweep: any of the undersigned left in it pays a share. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "The neck drew back. I waited, and raised my arms in its last instant. The others stood out of its path. Then I answered. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Maul. Heavy, on our knight. Wide sweep in front of her. Kept to her flank, out of the arc. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark mauled whoever held her, which was not me, never me. A wide sweep in front of her; I kept well to the side of it while our shield braced. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The great blow fell on our knight, and the sweep in front of the Hydra glowed the colour of trouble. I cast from her side, well out of it, while the knight braced. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Maul: on the knight, wide, in front. Knight braced; the rest of us out of the sweep. I swung in from her side, hot and fast, and out again before it fell. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The great blow came down on our knight with a wide sweep in front of her, and I went through her side and round the back of it, never in front, and out again before it landed.",
      marionettist: "The heavy blow is reserved for the knight, who takes it braced, with a wide sweep downstage of our leading lady. The company keeps out of the sweep. (My Doll works from her flank.)",
      cleric: "The great blow fell on our knight, and the sweep in front of the Mother caught our young archer, who knows better. I warded the knight before it landed; the rest of us keep out of that sweep now.",
      grovekeeper: "The heavy blow falls on our knight like a felled tree, with a wide sweep in front of her. I'd planted on him beforehand and he had his guard up. My lot keep out of the sweep.",
      chronomancer: "The heavy blow lands on our knight a beat after the Hydra rears, with a wide sweep before her. I warded him in the moment before; everyone else stayed outside the sweep. I will have been early. I was.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a heavy blow on whoever holds the specimen, and a wide sweep in front of her. Remedy: a ward on the holder, ready before; everyone else out of the sweep. Note: it clips the curious.",
      threadweaver: "The heavy blow came down on our knight, braced and ready, and ran down my thread so nobody took all of it. Everyone else keeps out of the sweep in front of her. A neat seam.",
    } },
    'Acid Spray': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head spat a cone of acid at one of the line, aimed where they stood when it began. It does not turn after. Step out sideways the moment it points, and keep off the burning middle after. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A head chose one of us and spat a cone at where they stood. It never turns to follow. We stepped out sideways as soon as it pointed, and stayed off the acid that lingered in its heart.",
      thresher: "A head sprays acid in a cone at where you stood when it started, and doesn't follow after. Step aside early, like stepping out of a furrow, and keep off the middle while it smokes.",
      bloodreaver: "A head spat acid at me in a great cone, aimed where I stood when it began. I wanted to taste it. Stepped sideways instead, and kept off the middle where it lingers. Barely a scratch, shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a head lowers and a cone is drawn at one of us, fixed where they stood. The answer: it does not follow, so step out sideways at once, and keep off the middle while it smokes. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head spat vinegar at one guest, aimed where they sat when it began, and it does not follow. Step sideways out of the cone the moment you see its line, and keep off the soured middle. Too sharp.",
      oathbroker: "The Spray is served at the address of record, where the party stood when it began; it is not amended after. The fine print: the middle stays acid a while. Step aside, and keep off it. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head looked at one of us and spat where they stood. It did not follow. Step aside, early. Do not walk back into the middle. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Acid cone at one of us. Aimed where you stood, doesn't track. Stepped out sideways early. Kept off the middle after. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head spat at me, aiming where I had been, which is never where I am. I slipped sideways the moment I saw its line, and stayed off the middle while it still steamed. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A head spat acid at me, aimed where I'd planted my feet when it began, and it never turned to follow! I stepped sideways at once and kept off the middle of it after, which stays nasty. Sleeves eaten, not burned. Novel.",
      thermist: "Acid cone. Aimed where I stood when it began; it does not follow. Step sideways, early. Then the hiss, the heat of it, the middle still burning a while, and I stayed off it. Too far, almost.",
      driftblade: "A head spat acid at where I'd been when it started, and it never turned to follow, so I was already through and out the side of it, and the middle stayed sour a while, so I went round that too.",
      marionettist: "A head spat acid at one of the company, aimed at their mark when it began; it does not follow. Step sideways on cue, and leave the middle of it alone a while. (My Doll's hem, alas.)",
      cleric: "A head spat acid at a friend, aimed where she stood when it began, and it never turned to follow. She stepped aside the moment she saw its aim, and kept off the burning middle after. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The acid comes like blight on the wind, aimed where one of my lot stood when it began, and it never turns. Step sideways at the first sign of it, and keep off the soured middle a while after.",
      chronomancer: "The acid is aimed on its first beat, at where its quarry stood, and never follows after. Step sideways on that beat; the middle keeps burning a few beats more. I will have kept off it. I did.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a cone of acid at one of us, aimed where they stood when it began; it does not follow. Remedy: step sideways the moment it points, and keep off the middle while it fizzes. Fine reagent, wasted on floors.",
      threadweaver: "A head spat acid at one stitch, aimed where she stood when it began. It does not follow, so sideways at once, and off the middle afterward; it stays sour a while. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Tidal Line': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head drove a lane of water straight across the temple, through one of the line. Narrow, and long. Whoever stood in it stepped off to either side, and the footing held. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The water ran in a straight lane across the temple, through one of the watch and out the far side. A step to either side and it passed us by, like a cart in the night.",
      thresher: "A head sends a straight lane of water across the temple, like a flood down an irrigation ditch. I stepped off the bank, either side, and let it run past.",
      bloodreaver: "A lane of water straight across the temple, right through me. I would have let it hit, but my friends were behind me. Stepped off it to the side. Wet, not wounded.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a lane drawn across the whole temple, through one of us. The answer: either side will serve; it has no width to speak of, so one step off it is enough. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head poured a long ribbon of broth straight across the temple, through one guest. Step to either side of the pour. Watery.",
      oathbroker: "The Line runs the whole length of the temple through one named party. The fine print is that it is narrow: either side of it is outside the claim. Step off. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Water spoke in a straight line across the temple. I stepped off it, to the side. It went on without me. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Water lane, straight across the temple, through one of us. Stepped off it, either side. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head threw a long lane of water through the room, through me, or where I had been. One step to the side and it passed. Drenching, not deadly. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A lane of water right across the temple, straight through my best spell! I stepped off it, either side will do, and finished from the dry. Mostly unsinged; entirely soaked, which is worse.",
      thermist: "Water lane, straight, through me. Off it, either side. Then I came back in steaming and furious and cut at the head that sent it. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "A lane of water came straight across the temple through me, and I went off it sideways and through the spray and round, and it was gone before I'd slowed.",
      marionettist: "A lane of water across the whole stage, through one of the company. Exit left or right; either wing will do. (The Doll was nearly washed into the orchestra.)",
      cleric: "A head drove a lane of water across the whole temple, straight through our brave knight. Either side of it was dry. He stepped off, a little late and a little wet. Dawn keep him.",
      grovekeeper: "A head sends a lane of water across the whole temple, like a ditch bursting its banks, straight through one of my lot. Step off to either side and it runs past. Plant first, wade never.",
      chronomancer: "A lane of water across the whole temple, through one of us, landing a beat after it's drawn. Either side is dry. I stepped off on the first beat, as I will have every time.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a lane of water straight across the temple, through one of us. Remedy: step off it, to either side. Note: wet labels peel. Keep the shelf off the lane, too.",
      threadweaver: "A lane of water cut straight across the temple through one stitch, like a ripped seam. Off it to either side, and back together after. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Venom Bloom': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head marked one of the line for poison we had to share. We closed in on them, every one of us but whoever stood in a ring, and split it. Then out of the bog it left. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The poison was marked for one, but meant for all of us to share. We gathered close on them and divided it; whoever kept a stump's ring was excused. Then we left the bog it made.",
      thresher: "Poison blooms on one of us, a load to share like a cart nobody pulls alone. All of us close round them, then out of the bog it leaves. Whoever's at a stump's ring stays at their work.",
      bloodreaver: "Poison marked on one of us, too big to take alone! We all piled in close and split it, a fine scar each. Then out of the bog it leaves. Whoever was searing a stump stayed there.",
      runeguard: "The omen: green rises over one of us. The answer: everyone gathers on the marked one so the blow is divided, save whoever holds a stump's ring; then out of the slowing bog it leaves. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A poisoned dish set before one guest, too rich for any stomach alone. We all sat in close and shared it, then left the table before the bog it leaves sucked us in. The one at the stump kept their seat. Bitter.",
      oathbroker: "The Bloom is a debt owed in common: the undersigned gather on the marked party and it is divided among us. The fine print excuses whoever is at a ring, and leaves a bog to step out of. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head spoke to one of us, meaning all. We gathered close and listened together. Then we stepped out of the mud it left. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Poison on one of us. Everyone in close, share it. Step out of the bog after. Stump-searer stays put. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A poison mark on one of us, to be shared, so I stood close with the others, which I hate. Then out of the bog, quickly. Whoever sears a stump is excused, the lucky thing. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Poison marked one of us, and it had to be split between all of us, so I gave up my lovely spot and huddled in close. Then out of the bog it left, quickly; it holds your feet. Whoever was searing a stump stayed put.",
      thermist: "Poison mark on one of us. Shared. Everyone close on them. Then the bog underfoot, slow and cold: out of it. The one at a stump's ring stays there. I ran hot, then cold. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "The poison mark had to be shared, so standing still with everyone, which I hate, so I circled until the last breath, slipped in among them, then straight out through the bog's edge before it held my feet.",
      marionettist: "Poison marks one player for a shared scene: the company crowds close and splits it, all but whoever minds a stump's ring. Then off the bog it leaves. (My Doll waded out last.)",
      cleric: "Poison marked a friend, too much for her alone. We gathered close on her and shared it out, all but whoever was keeping a stump's ring. Then we left the bog it made before it slowed us. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "The poison falls like a hard frost on a single plant, too much for it alone. So we gather in close round the marked one and share it, except whoever keeps a stump's ring. Then out of the bog it leaves.",
      chronomancer: "The poison lands a few beats after the mark. We gathered on the marked one in that gap and shared it, except whoever kept a stump's ring; then off the bog on the next beat. Nobody needed undoing.",
      alembist: "Ailment: poison on one of us, too strong a dose to take alone. Remedy: everyone close on the marked one to dilute it, except whoever keeps a stump's ring; then out of the bog it leaves. Diluted, nobody spoiled.",
      threadweaver: "The poison mark wanted sharing, so I drew every thread in close on the marked one and we split it. Then out of the bog, which snags at the ankles. Whoever was minding a ring stayed at it. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Flame Gout': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Fire came down on a pair of the line, a ring following each of them. They went far apart, so neither caught the other's, and the rest kept off the embers after. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Fire chose a pair of us, a ring that walked with each. They parted, far apart, each carrying only their own burning. The embers stayed on the floor a while after, small lights in the dark.",
      thresher: "Fire falls on a pair of us, each ring following like a dog at the heel. We walked far apart so each took only our own, and kept off the embers after, like a burnt field.",
      bloodreaver: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring each that follows! I wanted both. Instead my marked friend and I went far apart so nobody took both, and kept off the embers after. A fine scar.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a ring of fire hangs about a pair of us and follows them. The answer: they part, far apart, so neither ring touches the other; then the embers it leaves are avoided. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A flambe set on a pair of guests, each ring following its owner. Each carried their own plate far from the other, so nobody ate double, and left the embers to cool. Charred.",
      oathbroker: "The Gout names a pair of the undersigned, each liable only for their own ring. The fine print: if they stand together, each pays both. They parted, and kept off the embers after. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Fire spoke to a pair of us. They walked apart, far apart. Each carried only their own. Then we kept off the embers. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Fire on a pair of us. Rings follow. Split far apart, each takes only their own. Keep off the embers. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark set fire to a pair of us, and the rings followed us like admirers. We went to opposite sides so neither took both, and left the embers where they fell. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Fire on a pair of us, and none of it mine! Rude. The marked ones ran far apart so each ring burned only its owner, and I danced round the embers after. Jealous, not singed.",
      thermist: "Fire on a pair. Rings that follow. Apart, far apart, each alone with their own. I was marked and ran hot, gloriously, to the far side, and left embers I then had to avoid. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Fire on a pair of us, a ring following each, and I was one and ran the far way through the columns, away from the other ring, and left embers in my wake and never went back through them.",
      marionettist: "Fire falls on a pair of players, each followed by a ring of it. They exit to opposite ends of the stage and burn alone; embers stay on the boards a while. (Upstaged, frankly.)",
      cleric: "Fire fell on a pair of my flock, a ring following each. They went far apart so neither burned for the other, and I mended them before it landed. The embers stay a while; I walk round them.",
      grovekeeper: "Fire fell on a pair of my lot, a ring trailing each like wildfire after dry grass. They walked far apart so each burned only their own, and nobody lingered on the embers after.",
      chronomancer: "Fire on a pair of us, rings that follow, landing on the same beat. The pair walked far apart so neither caught the other's. The embers linger after, an echo on the floor. I stepped round the echo.",
      alembist: "Ailment: fire on a pair of us, a ring following each. Remedy: the pair go far apart so each takes only their own, then keep off the embers. Note: I once set a bench alight on purpose. Similar.",
      threadweaver: "Fire on a pair of stitches, each with a ring that follows. I cut the thread between them and sent them to opposite ends, then kept everyone off the embers. A clean cut.",
    } },
    'Rime Crown': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head threw a wide ring of ice round itself, with a quiet hollow right at its throat. I took the line in close, under the jaw, or well out past the ring. Never the band between. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "The ice ringed the head, wide and cold, but its throat stayed quiet. The watch stood close in under it or far outside. The band between is where the cold takes you.",
      thresher: "A head throws a ring of frost round itself, quiet right at its throat. Close in by the neck, or well past the ring's edge; the band between is the killing frost.",
      bloodreaver: "A head threw a ring of ice round itself, quiet at its very throat. I went right in, close under its jaw. The band between is where the cold bites. Not mine today.",
      runeguard: "The omen: frost circles a head, and the ground at its very throat stays clear. The answer: close in at the throat, or well beyond the ring; the band between is the danger. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head set a crown of ice round itself, with a warm little hollow right at its throat. I sat close in, under its chin; the others kept well outside the ring. Never the band between. Chilled.",
      oathbroker: "The Crown claims a broad ring round the head, but the fine print exempts the ground at its throat. Stand close, or stand well outside; the band between is forfeit. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head spoke ice round itself, but not at its throat. I stepped close, under its jaw. The cold passed around me. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Ice ring round a head. Quiet at its throat. Close in or well out, never the band between. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head crowned itself in ice, with a quiet hollow right at its throat. I slipped in close under its chin, the last place it would look. The band between is for the careless. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A head crowned itself in ice, with a quiet hollow right at its throat. I stood in the band between and froze my eyebrows, which I did not know could happen. Close in by the head, or well outside. Mostly unfrozen.",
      thermist: "Ice ring round the head. Quiet at its throat. Close in, or well out; never the band between. I liked the cold. I stood in the band once and liked it far less. Too far, the wrong way.",
      driftblade: "A head threw a ring of ice round itself, and I ran straight in through the band to the quiet hollow at its throat and cut it there, close in, because the band between is the only wrong place.",
      marionettist: "A head wears a crown of ice with a quiet hollow at its throat. I sent my Doll into the hollow and kept myself well beyond the ring. The band between is no place for anyone. (A cool performance.)",
      cleric: "A head crowned itself in ice, with a quiet hollow right at its throat. I learned to stand close by it, or well beyond the ring, never in the cold band between. Our archer found the band. Dawn keep her.",
      grovekeeper: "A head throws a ring of ice round itself like hoarfrost, but its throat stays quiet. Close in or well out; the band between is killing frost. My lot learned that in a single season.",
      chronomancer: "The ice ring lands on a single beat, with a quiet hollow at the head's throat. Close in, or well out; never the band between. I will have stood in the band only once. That was enough.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a wide ring of ice round a head, with a quiet hollow at its throat. Remedy: close in at the throat, or well outside the ring; not the band between. Store at the extremes.",
      threadweaver: "Ice round a head, with a quiet hollow at its throat. Close in or well out; the band between is where stitches freeze. I pulled one out of it by the collar. A sloppy seam.",
    } },
    'Stormcall': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head fanned lanes of lightning out from itself, the middle one through one of the line. The gaps between the lanes were good ground, and so was well to the side. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Lightning fanned from a head in lanes, the middle one through one of us. The dark between the lanes is safe; so is the ground off to the side. We kept to the gaps.",
      thresher: "A head fans lightning out in lanes, like rows in a field, the middle one through one of us. I stood in the gap between the rows, or well to the side, never in a lane.",
      bloodreaver: "A head fanned lightning out in lanes, the middle one through me! Stepped into a gap between them, which is safe, and so is well to the side. Only a tingle. Shameful.",
      runeguard: "The omen: lanes drawn out from a head like the ribs of a fan, the middle one through one of us. The answer: stand in the gaps between them, or well aside; never in a lane. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head served lightning in a fan of lanes, the middle one straight to a guest. The gaps between the lanes are clean plates, and so is well to the side. Fizzy.",
      oathbroker: "The Stormcall lays several lanes, the middle one served on one party. The fine print is that the gaps between the lanes are unclaimed ground. Stand there, or well aside. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head spoke lightning, in lanes. I stood in the silence between them. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Lightning fans out in lanes from a head. Middle one through one of us. Stood in a gap, or well to the side. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head fanned its lightning out in lanes, the middle one at me. I waited in the gap between them, where it is dark and safe. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A head fanned lanes of lightning out from itself, the middle one straight at me! I stepped into the gap between lanes, dark and safe, and my hair stood up anyway. Mostly unsinged, mostly unfrizzed.",
      thermist: "Lightning, a fan of lanes, the middle through me. Gaps are safe; so is well wide. I stepped into a gap and the air went hot either side of me, crackling, and missed. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Lightning fanned out in lanes from a head, the middle one through me, and I slipped sideways into the gap between and let it crack past either side, then through and round again.",
      marionettist: "A head fans lightning across the stage, the middle bolt for one of us. The dark between the bolts is safe, and so are the far wings. The company found its gaps on cue. (The Doll's hair, regrettably.)",
      cleric: "A head fanned lightning out in lanes, the middle aimed at a friend. The gaps between the lanes were quiet, and so was the ground well to the side. I stood in a gap and prayed through it. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "A head fans lightning out like a summer storm, the middle lane through one of my lot. Between the lanes the weather passes over, and well to the side as well. I stood in a gap. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Lightning fans out in lanes, the middle through one of us, all on the same beat. The gaps between are quiet, and so is well to the side. I stood in a gap and let the thunder keep time without me.",
      alembist: "Ailment: lightning in lanes fanned from a head, the middle through one of us. Remedy: stand in a gap between lanes, or well to the side. Note: lightning in a jar is less trouble than it looks.",
      threadweaver: "Lightning in a fan of lanes, the middle one through a stitch. The gaps between are safe, and so is well wide. I kept everyone in the gaps like thread between pins. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Stonegaze': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A head stared at everything it could see. The columns were our cover: I put the line behind one, with stone between us and that head. Behind its own column is nearest. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A head looked out over the temple, and what it saw turned to stone. We hid in the shadow of a column, stone between us and its eyes. Behind its own column is the nearest dark.",
      thresher: "A head looks out over the whole temple like a hard sun at noon. I stood in a column's shade, with the stone between us; the ground behind its own column is nearest.",
      bloodreaver: "A head looked at everything it could see, and I wanted to stare back. Put a column between us instead. Behind its own column was the nearest. No scar at all. Disappointing.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a head's eyes kindle. The answer: whatever it sees is taken, so a column must stand between you and it; the ground behind that head's own column is nearest. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head that looks too long at its guests turns them to stone, which spoils the appetite. I sat behind a column, out of its sight; the ground behind its own column is nearest. Stony.",
      oathbroker: "The Gaze claims everything in its sight. The fine print: a column is a lawful screen. We stood behind one, with the head's own column the nearest. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head looked at everything. I put a column between us, and it could not see me. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Gaze. Sees everything in line of sight. Put a column between me and the head. Its own column is nearest. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head looked at everything, which is my sort of game. I slipped behind a column, out of its eye; the one at its own back is nearest. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A head stared at everything it could see, and I am very easy to see! I hid behind a column with the stone between us; behind its own column is nearest. Undimmed, but sulking about it.",
      thermist: "A head, looking at everything. Column between us: the one behind its own is nearest. I stood in the open once and went cold to the bone, stiff. Too far out.",
      driftblade: "A head looked at everything it could see, which meant stopping behind a column with the stone between us, the one behind its own column nearest, and standing still, and I hated every moment of it.",
      marionettist: "A head gazes out at the entire house. The company hides behind the columns, scenery between them and the stare; the column behind the head's own is nearest. (My Doll missed its cue and stiffened.)",
      cleric: "A head looked over the whole temple, and whatever it saw went heavy and grey. The columns are our mercy: I put one between me and its eyes, and the nearest is just behind its own column. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "A head stares like the dead of winter, and all it sees turns grey. Shelter behind a column, the way sheep shelter behind a hedge. The nearest is the ground behind that head's own column.",
      chronomancer: "The gaze comes a few beats after the head lifts its eyes. A column between me and it is the only shelter, and the nearest is behind that head's own column. I will have been behind it already. I was.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a head's gaze, turning to stone whatever it sees. Remedy: a column between oneself and the head; the nearest is the ground behind that head's own column. Shelve behind stone.",
      threadweaver: "A head stared at all it could see. A column between it and my stitches; behind its own column is nearest. One stitch stood in the open and stiffened like starched linen. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Mire Spit': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "Bog came down on several of the line, a ring on each. We stepped out of our own rings and kept apart, then off the mire it left. Footing in that stuff is worthless. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "Bog fell on several of us, each with a ring of their own. We stepped out of them and kept apart, and then kept out of the slow mire it left behind.",
      thresher: "Bog falls on several of us, a ring each, like muck flung on a field. Out of my own ring, apart from the others, and off the mire after; it holds your boots like clay.",
      bloodreaver: "Bog came down on several of us, a ring each. Stepped out of mine, kept apart from my friends, then kept out of the mire. Slow and sticky. Not a scar worth the name.",
      runeguard: "The omen: rings settle on several of us at once. The answer: step out of your own, keep apart from the others, then leave the slowing mire alone. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "A head spat porridge onto several plates at once. Each guest stepped out of their own ring, kept apart, and stayed out of the sticky mess after. Stodgy.",
      oathbroker: "The Spit names several parties at once, a ring each. The fine print: the mire remains, and slows anyone who walks into it. Step out, keep apart, keep off. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "Mud spoke to several of us. We stepped out, each alone. We did not walk back into it. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Bog rings on several of us. Out of your own, apart from the others. Keep off the mire after. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A head spat bog at several of us. I stepped out of my ring, well away from anyone else's, and kept out of the mire it left, which is no place for good boots. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Bog fell on several of us, a ring each. I stepped out of mine, away from the others, and then kept off the mire it left, which is no stage for a fire mage. Mostly unsinged, very muddy.",
      thermist: "Mire on several. A ring each. Out of my own, away from the others. Then the cold slow mud it leaves: off it. I ran hot with impatience, wading. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "Bog fell on several of us, a ring under each, and I ran out of mine away from the others and round the mire it left, which grabs at your feet, never landing in it.",
      marionettist: "Bog rains on several of the company, a ring for each. Each steps out of their own, apart, and stays off the mire after. (A muddy scene. Cut in rehearsal, next time.)",
      cleric: "Bog fell on several of my flock, a ring on each. Each stepped out of their own and kept apart from the others, then out of the mire it left. Our knight lingered in his. I scolded him gently.",
      grovekeeper: "Bog fell like heavy rain on several of my lot, a ring on each. They stepped out of their own and kept apart, then off the mire after; it holds feet like wet clay. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Bog falls on several of us at once, a ring each. We stepped out of our own rings on the beat, kept apart, then stayed out of the mire, which slows the next beat to a crawl.",
      alembist: "Ailment: bog on several of us, a ring on each. Remedy: out of one's own ring, apart from the others, then clear of the mire after; it slows. Label: do not steep.",
      threadweaver: "Bog fell on several stitches, a ring on each. Each out of their own, apart from the others, then off the mire, which holds like wet wool. Tied them back together after.",
    } },
    'Searing': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "A cut head left a bleeding stump and a blue ring at its column's foot. I stood in the ring until it closed, and it sealed. Miss it and a pair grow back. Cut the gentlest first. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "When a head falls, a blue ring opens at the foot of its column. Someone must keep watch in it until it closes, or a pair of heads rise from the stump. I took my turn. The gentlest goes first.",
      thresher: "Cut a head and the stump bleeds; a blue ring opens at the column behind. Stand in it till it closes and the stump sears, or a pair sprout like weeds. The first head cut grows back late, so cut the mildest first.",
      bloodreaver: "Cut a head and the stump bleeds, and a blue ring opens at the foot of the column behind it. I stood in it until it closed and the stump seared. Miss it and a pair sprout. Kill the gentlest head first.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a stump bleeds and a blue ring opens at its column. The answer: one of us stands in it until it closes; and since the first cut returns larger at the end, the gentlest is cut first. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "Every head we carve leaves a raw stump, and a blue ring opens at the column behind it. I stood in it until it closed and seared the cut. Leave it and a pair of small ones sprout. Carve the mildest first.",
      oathbroker: "Each cut head is a debt not yet settled until the stump is seared: someone stands in the blue ring until it closes. Default, and a pair grow in its place. Fine print: the first cut is reissued, enlarged. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A head fell silent. A ring opened at its column. I stood in it until it closed. The stump did not speak again. Cut the quietest one first. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Cut a head, stump bleeds, blue ring opens at the column behind. Someone stands in it till it closes, or a pair sprout. Softest head first. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "Each head we took left a bleeding stump and a blue ring at the column behind it. I stood in it until it closed and the stump seared; neglect it and a pair grow back. Gentlest head first. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A cut stump bled, and a blue ring opened at the foot of the column behind it. I stood in it till it closed and seared the stump shut; left empty, a pair of little heads sprout. Gentlest head first, always. Glorious.",
      thermist: "Cut stump, blue ring at the column behind it. Stand in it until it closes. Hot work, searing. Miss it: a pair of small heads. And gentlest first, because the first comes back bigger. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "When a head comes off the stump bleeds and a blue ring opens at the column behind it, and someone stands in it until it closes, and that someone was me, still, outraged, or a pair of heads sprouts. Gentlest first, too.",
      marionettist: "A cut head leaves a bleeding stump and a blue ring at the foot of the column upstage of it. Someone stands in it until it closes, or a pair of understudies sprouts. And cast off the gentlest first. (Noted in the prompt book.)",
      cleric: "When a head is cut, its stump bleeds and a blue ring opens at the column behind. Someone must stand there until it closes, or a pair of heads sprout. We cut the gentlest head first, for the first returns. Sear every stump.",
      grovekeeper: "Cut a head and the stump weeps, and a blue ring opens at the column behind. Someone stands in it till it closes, or a pair of heads sucker up. Prune the gentlest first: the first cut grows back.",
      chronomancer: "Cut a head and its stump bleeds; a blue ring opens at the column behind. Someone stands in it until it closes, or a pair of heads sprout. The first head cut comes back bigger at the end, so the gentlest goes first. I'll have remembered.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a cut head leaves a bleeding stump; a blue ring opens at the column behind. Remedy: someone stands in it until it closes, or a pair of heads sprout. Note: the first cut returns bigger; cut the gentlest first.",
      threadweaver: "A cut neck bleeds, and a blue ring opens at the foot of the column behind it. Someone stands in it until it closes and sears the stump, or a pair of small heads sprouts. Gentlest first. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Submerge': { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "She dived and the water rose. Slow wading, bad footing. Heads came up at the columns one after another, a ring showing each. The line stayed out of the rings and cut each head before it struck. Gave ground, held.",
      gloomsworn: "She sank, and the temple drowned. We waded slowly while heads broke the surface at the columns, one after another, a ring marking each. We kept out of the rings and cut each head before it struck.",
      thresher: "She went under and the temple flooded like a field in spring; we waded. Heads come up at the columns one after another, each with a ring first. Off the ring, and cut the head before it strikes.",
      bloodreaver: "She dove and the temple flooded, and we waded like old men. Heads broke the surface at the columns, each showing its ring first. Stayed out of the ring, cut the head before it struck. Good fun.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she sinks and the water rises. The answer: each head shows a ring where it will rise; stay out of it, then cut it down before it strikes. The old heads wait below meanwhile. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "My hostess dove into her soup and flooded the dining hall; everyone waded. Heads rose at the columns, each ring showing where. I stayed off the ring and ate the head before it bit. Wet, and worth it.",
      oathbroker: "The Dive is a recess with penalties: the temple floods, and heads come up at named columns, each with a ring. The fine print: stand out of the ring, then discharge the head before it strikes. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She went under. The water rose to our knees. Heads rose where the rings showed. I stood aside, then answered each before it spoke. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Dive. Temple floods, slow wading. Heads come up at columns, ring shows where. Out of the ring, cut it before it strikes. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark dove and flooded the room, which made sneaking very slow. Heads came up at the columns, each ring showing where. I kept off the ring and cut the head before it could strike. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "She dove and the temple flooded to my knees, and I waded like a soggy candle! Heads rose at the columns, each with a ring where it would break the water. I kept out of the ring and burned the head down before it struck.",
      thermist: "Dive. Water to the waist, slow, cold. Heads rise at the columns, a ring under each before it breaks. Out of the ring. Then hot, fast: cut it down before it strikes. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "She dove and the temple flooded and I could not run, only wade, which is a kind of prison, and heads came up at the columns, a ring showing each, so I kept out of the ring and cut through the neck before it struck.",
      marionettist: "Our leading lady exits below, the stage floods, and the company wades. Heads rise at the columns, each with a ring marking its entrance. Keep off the mark, and cut it before it delivers its line. (The Doll swam.)",
      cleric: "The Mother dove and the temple flooded; we waded like pilgrims. Heads rose at the columns, each showing a ring where it would break. We kept out of the ring, then cut the head before it bit. Dawn keep us dry.",
      grovekeeper: "She dove and the temple flooded like a spring meadow; we waded. Heads break the surface at the columns, each showing a ring first. My lot keep out of the ring and cut the head down before it strikes.",
      chronomancer: "She dives, the temple floods, and everyone wades at a slower tempo. Heads rise at the columns beat after beat, each with a ring first: out of the ring, then cut it down before it strikes. The old heads wait below.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the specimen dives and the temple floods; everyone wades. Symptoms: heads rising at the columns, a ring showing where each will break. Remedy: out of the ring, then cut the head before it strikes.",
      threadweaver: "She dove and the temple flooded, and my stitches waded like wet hems. Heads rose at the columns, each showing a ring first; keep out of it, and cut the head before it strikes. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    "Mother's Heart": { hard: false, learned: {
      bastion: "At the end she sealed herself. Every head we cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then all the living ones at once. I knew the order and moved the line clear of each. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "At the last she shut herself away. Every head we had cut rose from its stump in the order we cut them, then the living struck together. I kept the order in mind, like the hours of a long watch.",
      thresher: "She sealed herself at the end, and every head we cut struck again from its stump, in the order we reaped them. Then the living ones together. Remember the order you cut, and stand clear of each.",
      bloodreaver: "At the end she sealed herself and I could not touch her! Every head we cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then the live ones all at once. Knew our order, stood clear of each.",
      runeguard: "The omen: she is sealed, and the stumps stir. The answer: they strike in the order we cut them, so the order must be known; then every living head at once; then the heart beats, and the seal opens. As foreseen.",
      gorgemaw: "For dessert my hostess sealed herself away. Every head we carved struck again from its stump, in the order we carved them, then the living ones together. I remembered the order of courses. Then her heart.",
      oathbroker: "The Heart calls in every cut head's account, in the order of cutting, then every living head at once. The fine print is our own ledger: know the order, read each stump, stand clear of each. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "She closed. Her cut heads spoke again, in the order we silenced them. Then all the living spoke together. I remembered the order. Then her heart opened. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Sealed. Cut heads strike from their stumps in the order we cut them. Then the live ones together. Knew the order, read each stump, stood clear. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "The mark sealed herself, which was very rude. Every head we cut struck again from its stump in the order we took them, then the living ones all at once. I remembered. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "Sealed, unburnable, insulting! Every stump struck again in the order we'd cut them, so I kept the order in my head and stood clear of each at its turn. Then the living heads all at once, then her heart. Barely singed.",
      thermist: "Sealed. Nothing to cut. Every stump strikes again, in the order we cut them: remember it, cold. Clear of each in turn. Then the living heads together, all hot at once. Then the heart. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "She sealed herself and every stump we'd cut struck again in the order we'd cut them, so I ran the order like a route, clear of each at its turn, then clear of every living head at once, then through to her heart.",
      marionettist: "The finale is a revival: every head we cut returns from its stump, in the order we cut them, so the company must remember its own blocking. Then all the living heads at once, then the heart. (Bravo.)",
      cleric: "At the end she sealed herself, and every head we had cut struck again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then all the living at once. I kept our order like a prayer and moved my flock each time.",
      grovekeeper: "At the last she sealed herself, and every stump struck again in the order we cut them, then the living heads all together. We reaped what we sowed, in the order we sowed it. I kept the order and moved my lot each time.",
      chronomancer: "The ending is an echo: every head we cut strikes again from its stump, in the order we cut them, then every living head at once. I kept the order like a melody and stood clear of each note on its turn.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the specimen sealed; every head we cut strikes again from its stump in the order cut, then every living head at once. Remedy: a written record of the order, each stump read, clear of each at its turn. Labels matter.",
      threadweaver: "Sealed, untouchable. Every stump struck again in the very order we cut them, so I kept the order like a pattern and moved my stitches clear of each in turn. Then every living head at once, then her heart. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    'Regrowth': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "On the harder ground, a missed ring grew back more heads than before. Nobody leaves a ring early now. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "On the harder nights, a missed searing brings up yet another head. No ring goes unwatched now. The dark remembers.",
      thresher: "On the hard night a missed searing sprouts yet more, like weeds left to seed. Never leave a stump unburned.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard night a missed ring grew even more heads than before. Far too many for my liking, and I like a lot. Never miss a searing.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a ring left empty. The answer, on the harder road, is worse: more heads than before rise from it. No ring is left unwatched. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder menu a missed searing serves yet another head on top. Far too many courses even for me. Every stump seared, every time.",
      oathbroker: "On the harder terms, a missed searing accrues interest: yet another head on top of the usual pair. The fine print is that no ring may go unattended. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "On the harder road, an unanswered stump said more than before. Answer every ring. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard. Missed searing grows another head on top. Never miss the ring. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "On the harder night a missed ring grew still more heads, which is greedy of it. Every stump seared, without exception. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair! I stood in every ring after that until it closed, even mid-spell. Especially mid-spell. Mostly unsinged.",
      thermist: "Hard. A missed ring grows yet another head besides the pair. Cold lesson: never miss the ring. I ran scalding at whoever wandered off one. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair, so I stopped grumbling about standing still and stood in every ring until it closed, which was the stillest I have ever been.",
      marionettist: "On the hard night an empty ring grew yet another head besides the pair. Nobody misses the ring now; it is the one cue that cannot be dropped. (The understudies are getting out of hand.)",
      cleric: "On the harder night, a stump left unseared sprouted not merely a pair of heads but another besides. I will never let a ring stand empty again. Dawn forgive me that one.",
      grovekeeper: "On the harder night, a stump left unseared came up thicker than ever, more heads than the usual pair. Never leave a ring empty; it is like leaving bindweed in the bed.",
      chronomancer: "On the harder night a missed ring grew the usual pair and another besides. I will have stood in every ring after that. I did; I am not undoing that many heads again.",
      alembist: "Ailment, harder night: a missed ring grows a pair of heads and another besides. Remedy: none after the fact. Prevention only: sear every stump. Underlined, heavily.",
      threadweaver: "On the hard night a missed ring grew yet another head besides the pair. No stitch of mine leaves a ring before it closes now. Mind the slack.",
    } },
    'Tidal Dance': { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "A pair of heads crossed their water lanes over and over, each pass a step along. The wedges between the crossing lanes are never painted. The line moved to the next wedge each time. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "A pair of heads crossed their lanes of water, again and again, each time a step along. The wedges between were left dark and safe. We walked to the next one each time.",
      thresher: "A pair of heads cross their water lanes, a step round each time, like ploughing in a turning pattern. The wedges between stay dry and are never painted. I moved to the next each time.",
      bloodreaver: "A pair of heads crossed their lanes of water over and over, a step along each time. The wedges between are safe, and never painted. Moved to the next wedge each time. A dance, and I loved it.",
      runeguard: "The omen: a pair of lanes drawn across each other, shifting a step each time. The answer: the wedges between them are never painted; step into the next wedge with every crossing. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "A pair of heads poured their broth across each other, again and again, a step along each time. The wedges between the pours stay dry. I moved to the next wedge each course. Dizzying.",
      oathbroker: "The Dance is a series of crossing claims, each shifted a step along. The fine print: the wedges between the lanes are never claimed. Move to the next wedge each time. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "A pair of heads spoke across each other, over and over, moving each time. I stepped with them, into the quiet wedge. Breathe.",
      ranger: "A pair of heads cross their lanes, step along each time. Wedges between stay safe, never painted. Moved to the next wedge. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "A pair of heads crossed their water lanes over and over, stepping along each time. The wedges between are never painted and never struck. I drifted to the next one each time. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "A pair of heads crossed their water lanes again and again, a step along each time! The wedges between were never painted, so I hopped wedge to wedge like a fire on a damp night. Drenched, unbowed.",
      thermist: "A pair of lanes, crossing, again and again, a step along each time. The wedges between are never painted. Wedge to wedge, cold and exact, while the water roared past me. Back to the middle.",
      driftblade: "A pair of heads crossed their water lanes over and over, a step along each time, and the wedges between were never painted, so I slipped through from wedge to wedge, round and round, never landing in the water.",
      marionettist: "A pair of heads cross their lanes of water again and again, a step along each time. The wedges between are never lit; the company moves wedge to wedge on each cue. (Choreography! At last.)",
      cleric: "A pair of heads crossed their water lanes over and over, a step along each time. The wedges between were never painted and stayed dry. I led my flock from wedge to wedge, like stepping stones. Thank the light.",
      grovekeeper: "A pair of heads crossed their lanes of water again and again, a step along each time, like a plough turning furrows. The wedges between were never painted. My lot stepped from wedge to wedge.",
      chronomancer: "A pair of lanes crossing, again and again, each crossing a step along from the last. The wedges between are never painted. A wedge for each beat, like a dance; I will have trodden on nobody's feet.",
      alembist: "Ailment: a pair of heads crossing water lanes, again and again, a step along each time. Remedy: the wedges between are never painted; move to the next wedge at every crossing. Shelved under dancing.",
      threadweaver: "A pair of lanes crossing, a step along each time, like cross-stitch. The wedges between are never painted. Wedge to wedge, every stitch together. Pulled it tight.",
    } },
    "Hydra's Gaze": { hard: true, learned: {
      bastion: "Every eye she has watched the middle of the temple at once. Only the ground by the outer stones was safe. The line had its back to the edge before it ended. Held.",
      gloomsworn: "All her eyes opened on the heart of the temple at once. Only the ground at the edge stayed in shadow. We set our backs to the outer stone before it closed.",
      thresher: "On the hard night the whole middle of the temple was watched, like noon with no shade. Only the ground by the wall was spared. Back to it before the look ended.",
      bloodreaver: "On the hard night every head watched the whole middle at once. Only the ground by the wall was safe. Back to the wall, before it ended. No scar. Disappointing.",
      runeguard: "The omen: every head turns inward at once. The answer: the whole middle is watched, and only the ground by the outer stones is safe; be there before it ends. Not foreseen; now it is.",
      gorgemaw: "On the harder menu the whole table is watched at once, and only the seats by the wall are spared. Back to the wall, before the look ends. Cold service.",
      oathbroker: "The Gaze claims the whole middle of the temple at once. The fine print exempts only the ground by the outer stones. We stood there, backs to the edge. So noted.",
      ironpalm: "All her eyes spoke at once, to the middle. I went to the edge and put my back to the stone. Breathe.",
      ranger: "Hard. Whole middle watched. Ground by the wall's safe. Back to the wall before it ends. Clean line.",
      shadowblade: "On the hard night the mark watched the whole middle at once, which was unfair even for her. Only the ground by the wall was hidden. My back was to it before she finished. It never saw me.",
      pyromancer: "The whole middle of the temple was watched at once, and the brightest thing in it was me. I ran for the outer stones and put my back to the wall before it ended. Undimmed. Mostly.",
      thermist: "The whole middle, watched. The middle is wrong, for once. Back to the outer wall before it ends. I ran hot at the irony, cold at the stare, and stood with my back to the stones. Too far is right, tonight.",
      driftblade: "The whole middle of the temple was watched at once, and I ran straight out through it to the outer stones and put my back to the wall before it ended, and stood there, still, furious, alive.",
      marionettist: "The whole of centre stage is watched at once. The company retreats to the back of the house, against the temple wall, before it ends. (I was already there. I always am.)",
      cleric: "Her eyes took in the whole middle of the temple at once; only the ground by the wall was safe. I gathered my flock with their backs to the stones before it ended. Dawn keep us.",
      grovekeeper: "Her stare came over the whole middle of the temple like a killing frost over open field. Only the ground by the wall was spared. My lot put their backs to it before it ended. Plant first.",
      chronomancer: "Her gaze takes the whole middle of the temple at once, a few beats after she lifts her eyes. Only the ground by the wall is safe. Next time, which was last time, I'll be there before it ends.",
      alembist: "Ailment: the whole middle of the temple watched at once. Remedy: backs to the wall before it ends; only that ground is safe. Note: the shelves were against the wall all along. Clever shelves.",
      threadweaver: "The whole middle of the temple watched at once. Only by the wall is safe, so I hemmed everyone to the edge, backs to the stones, before it ended. Mind the slack.",
    } },
  },
};
